ent Type: Personal Profile', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-04T09:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Kaiser-Schatzlein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaiser-Schatzlein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/999e0fac-6a3c-5f1d-ad48-ad081fea722a', 'word_count': 1199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/999e0fac-6a3c-5f1d-ad48-ad081fea722a'}, {'abstract': 'Americans who were incarcerated or have a conviction on their record are largely locked out of the economy. This moral outrage must end. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/04/opinion/clean-slate-incarceration-work.html', 'snippet': 'Americans who were incarcerated or have a conviction on their record are largely locked out of the economy. This moral outrage must end. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'One in three American adults — more than 70 million people — have some type of arrest or criminal record. To put this in perspective, about the same number of Americans have college degrees right now.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/04/opinion/04dimon/04dimon-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/04/opinion/04dimon/04dimon-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/04/opinion/04dimon/04dimon-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/04/opinion/04dimon/04dimon-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/04/opinion/04dimon/04dimon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/04/opinion/04dimon/04dimon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/04/opinion/04dimon/04dimon-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'If You Paid Your Debt to Society, You Should Be Allowed to Work', 'kicker': 'Guest Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jobs Bring Dignity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ex-Convicts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blunt Rochester, Lisa', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Casey, Robert P Jr', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lightfoot, Lori', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-04T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jamie Dimon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jamie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dimon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/b63146a6-3af7-5a09-8370-e3d413e7da41', 'word_count': 1109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b63146a6-3af7-5a09-8370-e3d413e7da41'}, {'abstract': 'A first birthday calls for these small tender confections, just right for chubby baby hands.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/04/magazine/mini-strawberry-cakes-recipe.html', 'snippet': 'A first birthday calls for these small tender confections, just right for chubby baby hands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Almost a year ago, Gemma Tao Greenspan was born, and I became a grandmother. I also became the person you couldn’t trust to remember a thing about her own child’s life. When my daughter-in-law, Linling Tao, called to ask when Joshua, my son and her husband, first slept through the night, I didn’t know the answer and couldn’t guess at it. First step? I think it was 11 months. First real food? I don’t remember. I hope it wasn’t cookies, but it might have been. I remember that he did a terrific imitation of a flamingo at 15 months — I know this because we recorded it on our video camera, which weighed almost as much as I did. But did he like green beans? Cod? Chicken livers? Chicken livers! I never thought to make them for Joshua, but I probably should have — all that iron would have been good for him. Taking care of a baby was so full time, so all-everything for me that I couldn’t imagine I’d ever forget a minute of Joshua’s life. But I was wrong.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/08/magazine/08mag-eat/08mag-eat-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 452, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/08/magazine/08mag-eat/08mag-eat-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 452}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/08/magazine/08