rson': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Small', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/979137dd-827a-5b72-a3e2-a2ecf7ac9306', 'word_count': 1381, 'uri': 'nyt://article/979137dd-827a-5b72-a3e2-a2ecf7ac9306'}, {'abstract': 'As a child, Theary Seng escaped Cambodia’s killing fields. After returning there as a human rights advocate, she angered the country’s strongman leader. But she refuses to be driven away again.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/06/world/asia/cambodia-genocide-theary-seng.html', 'snippet': 'As a child, Theary Seng escaped Cambodia’s killing fields. After returning there as a human rights advocate, she angered the country’s strongman leader. But she refuses to be driven away again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BANGKOK — Only a small child when the Khmer Rouge seized power in 1975 and soon made her an orphan, Theary Seng has two enduring memories from that time in Cambodia’s tortured history.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/06/world/00seng-HP/merlin_182313360_0b2ae6f3-e64e-4459-b5ca-699cdc900729-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/06/world/00seng-HP/merlin_182313360_0b2ae6f3-e64e-4459-b5ca-699cdc900729-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/06/world/00seng-HP/merlin_182313360_0b2ae6f3-e64e-4459-b5ca-699cdc900729-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/06