], 'pub_date': '2021-08-07T01:56:02+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Reuters', 'person': [], 'organization': 'Reuters'}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/2db5e07c-368a-52b5-81fb-4ec80c7fbefd', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/2db5e07c-368a-52b5-81fb-4ec80c7fbefd'}, {'abstract': 'With Adam Aaronson as chief mixologist, you can be sure of only one thing: Surprises are in store.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/06/crosswords/daily-puzzle-2021-08-07.html', 'snippet': 'With Adam Aaronson as chief mixologist, you can be sure of only one thing: Surprises are in store.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SATURDAY PUZZLE — I had a tough time finding a way into Adam Aaronson’s benignly fiendish Saturday puzzle. Some constructors, and Mr. Aaronson seems to be one of them, excel at writing clues that have the precision necessary for the right answer to click smartly into place when it is entered, yet are just vague enough to have seven or 12 or 29 of those potential right answers. Frustrating? Yup. Fun? Totally. Slow? Exceptionally, but maybe that’s just me. Rewarding? Very much so. As I filled in and deleted and filled in and deleted (happily, I wasn’t tethered to a pen or pencil) some almost-poetic patterns revealed themselves.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/07/arts/07WORDPLAY-ONE-EYED/merlin_81332264_81c85613-22b1-48dd-bbe3-f48ab4f1acc2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/07/arts/07WORDPLAY-ONE-EYED/merlin_81332264_81c85613-22b1-48dd-bbe3-f48ab4f1acc2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/07/arts/07WORDPLAY-ONE-EYED/merlin_81332264_81c85613