reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e55bba21-8c44-5fe2-8769-149b28110e4b', 'word_count': 1331, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e55bba21-8c44-5fe2-8769-149b28110e4b'}, {'abstract': 'That this perennial wildflower digests trapped insects suggests that other plants’ appetites for animals may be overlooked.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/09/science/carnivorous-flower-plant.html', 'snippet': 'That this perennial wildflower digests trapped insects suggests that other plants’ appetites for animals may be overlooked.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This wildflower looks innocent. Found in wetlands not far from major cities in the Pacific Northwest, it lures in pollinators with white blossoms atop a long, sticky stem. You can even buy seeds of the Western false asphodel in garden stores.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/17/science/09tb-carnivore-2/merlin_193098615_17cee61b-5793-4019-9474-c02f68084fbd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/17/science/09tb-carnivore-2/merlin_193098615_17cee61b-5793-4019-9474-c02f68084fbd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/17/science/09tb-carnivore-2/merlin_193098615_17cee61b-5793-4019-9474-c02f68084fbd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/17/science/09tb-carnivore-2/merlin_193098615_17cee61b-5793-4019-9474-c02f68084fbd-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/17/science/09tb-carnivore-2/09tb-carnivore-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/17/science/09tb-carnivore-2/09tb-carnivore-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/17/science/09tb-carnivore-2/09tb-carnivore-2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'This Flower Hides a Secret: It’s Actually a Carnivore', 'kicker': 'Trilobites', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Big Appetite: This Flower Hides a Secret:  It’s Actually a Carnivore', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insects', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evolution (Biology)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pacific Northwestern States (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'your-feed-science', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of British Columbia', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-09T19:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Asher Elbein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Asher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elbein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/521d8a2e-c2e7-59f2-8406-d1df6577f803', 'word_count': 831, 'uri': 'nyt://article/521d8a2e-c2e7-59f2-8406-d1df6577f803'}, {'abstract': 'Marvelous by Fred in Midtown is a French pastry shop from Frédéric Vaucamps featuring seven iterations of the merveilleux.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/09/dining/marvelous-by-fred-pastries.html', 'snippet': 'Marvelous by Fred in Midtown is a French pastry shop from Frédéric Vaucamps featuring seven iterations of the merveilleux.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The merveilleux, the signature pastry made by Frédéric Vaucamps in Lille, France, has been available in his small shop on Jane Street in the West Village for many years. Now the company, which has 46 shops worldwide, has opened a second New York store, a flagship in Midtown named for the pastry. The feather-light cake and meringue confection, in seven flavors, comes in regular and mini sizes. Brioches, croissants and sandwiches are also sold. The shop, with late 18th-century décor that echoes the post-revolutionary era during which the pastry is said to have been created, includes an enormous crystal chandelier and has seating on two levels indoors.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-cake/09Burner-cake-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-cake/09Burner-cake-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-cake/09Burner-cake-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-cake/09Burner-cake-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-cake/09Burner-cake-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-cake/09Burner-cake-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-cake/09Burner-cake-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A New Home for Wondrous Pastries', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Nibble: Wondrous Pastries Find a New Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bakeries and Baked Products', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marvelous by Fred (Manhattan, NY, Bakery)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-09T19:16:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e7cea10-8238-5f40-a106-cb08405c5295', 'word_count': 144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e7cea10-8238-5f40-a106-cb08405c5295'}, {'abstract': 'A new line of table linens from a Los Angeles company adds breezy elegance to summer meals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/09/dining/morrow-soft-goods.html', 'snippet': 'A new line of table linens from a Los Angeles company adds breezy elegance to summer meals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Checked tablecloths and napkins are common in your neighborhood bistro, red sauce joint or summer picnic basket. But at Morrow Soft Goods, a Los Angeles company owned by Michelle Toney and Stephanie Cleary, the gingham for its line of 100 percent linen table wares is subtler than the typical red, in tones like honey and cream. Until now, the company has specialized in linen bedding. The new kitchen and dining collection features napkins and place mats ($55 to $70 for four), tablecloths ($119 to $125), and aprons ($50 to $55) in checks, and in earthy solids like sage, terra cotta and apricot, colors that will easily take you into fall.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-linen/merlin_192790020_8c9ea748-518c-49ac-bfc1-d07c1b737923-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-linen/merlin_192790020_8c9ea748-518c-49ac-bfc1-d07c1b737923-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-linen/merlin_192790020_8c9ea748-518c-49ac-bfc1-d07c1b737923-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-linen/merlin_192790020_8c9ea748-518c-49ac-bfc1-d07c1b737923-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-linen/09Burner-linen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-linen/09Burner-linen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-linen/09Burner-linen-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Keep It Casual in Earth Tones', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Display: Keeping It Casual With Earth Tones', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tableware', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-09T19:19:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4432e957-dae8-50df-8366-7060eeff89fe', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4432e957-dae8-50df-8366-7060eeff89fe'}, {'abstract': 'Some of the herbaceous extra-virgin olive oils made in Tuscany are among the best the founder of the Rare Wine Company has tasted.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/09/dining/tuscan-olive-oil.html', 'snippet': 'Some of the herbaceous extra-virgin olive oils made in Tuscany are among the best the founder of the Rare Wine Company has tasted.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Olive oil is made all over Italy, but no region produces oils as singular as those from Tuscany. Mannie Berk, the president of the Rare Wine Company in Brisbane, Calif., is a connoisseur who has had Tuscan extra-virgin olive oils bottled for his company since 1995. Lately, he has found them lacking their customary verve and intensity, a shortcoming he attributes to global warming. But not those made in 2020. He raves about the vintage. Though his company imports 22 different oils, some are worth knowing, like leccino oils by Pruneti, a classic; and Sàgona, more forceful, $59.99 for both. A new oil for his company is the herbaceous Fonte di Foiano 1979 from the Bolgheri region, sold as part of a set of six from areas in and around Chianti for $157.50. Also in that set is the Volpaia from the Classico district, a blend of several olives that’s deep green, minty and viscous with bracing bitterness. The oils, all sold in half-liter bottles, are to use for finishing, not sautéing, to elevate a bowl of pappa al pomodoro or grilled fennel, or to trace over slices of rosemary-scented pork roast.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09burner-oil/09burner-oil-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09burner-oil/09burner-oil-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09burner-oil/09burner-oil-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09burner-oil/09burner-oil-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09burner-oil/09burner-oil-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09burner-oil/09burner-oil-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09burner-oil/09burner-oil-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At Least One Thing Good Came Out of 2020', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Drizzle: Tuscan Olive Oil Has a Stellar 2020', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olive Oil', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rare Wine Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tuscany (Italy)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-09T19:22:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b407bb78-8445-5a0f-84b6-eab713948683', 'word_count': 225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b407bb78-8445-5a0f-84b6-eab713948683'}, {'abstract': 'For her new book, “Why We Cook,” the illustrator Lindsay Gardner spoke to more than 100 women about the allure of food.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/09/dining/why-we-cook-book.html', 'snippet': 'For her new book, “Why We Cook,” the illustrator Lindsay Gardner spoke to more than 100 women about the allure of food.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Through a series of profiles, reminiscences, essays, interviews and recipes, “Why We Cook” explores many facets of women in the world of food. More than 100 chefs, writers, entrepreneurs, farmers, wine experts and educators contributed to this lively book. The bright illustrations are by the author, Lindsay Gardner, who seeks to understand exactly why she turned to the kitchen; she thought others could address that question, too. Reem Assil, Amanda Cohen, Joyce Goldstein, Christine Ha, Deborah Madison, Jen Pelka, Ruth Reichl, Jordan Salcito and Bonnie Tsui are among the women whose opinions appear. It’s a book to browse, as I’ve been doing over the past few months.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09burner-book1/09burner-book1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09burner-book1/09burner-book1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09burner-book1/09burner-book1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09burner-book1/09burner-book1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09burner-book1/09burner-book1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09burner-book1/09burner-book1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09burner-book1/09burner-book1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Book to Inspire in the Kitchen', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Consult: A Book to Inspire In the Kitchen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Why We Cook: Women on Food, Identity and Connection (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gardner, Lindsay (Illustrator)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-09T19:26:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3fe6a5f-bc22-5f08-9111-22132724695a', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3fe6a5f-bc22-5f08-9111-22132724695a'}, {'abstract': 'A lower-sodium salt substitute from the Baja Peninsula of Mexico is made with salicornia, a halophyte.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/09/dining/green-salt.html', 'snippet': 'A lower-sodium salt substitute from the Baja Peninsula of Mexico is made with salicornia, a halophyte.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Your salt wardrobe may already include black and pink. But what about green? A new naturally green salt substitute produced from salicornia, a halophyte, has hit the market. Decades ago, José Ramón Noriega planted salicornia on salt-affected farmland in northern Baja California, Mexico. Now his sons, Paul, Erick and Irving Noriega, and another partner, Chris Lin, are cultivating the crop organically, dehydrating and pulverizing it to make a salty powder they call Green Salt. It’s salty enough to use in place of table salt, though it’s substantially lower in sodium. It adds a nice touch of vegetal flavor when dusted atop a deviled egg, baked potato or sautéed fish. That flavor disappears in cake batter or pasta sauce. While Green Salt is more costly than regular kosher or sea salt, it’s comparable in price to many boutique salts.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-Salt/09Burner-Green-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-Salt/09Burner-Green-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-Salt/09Burner-Green-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-Salt/09Burner-Green-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-Salt/09Burner-Green-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-Salt/09Burner-Green-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-Salt/09Burner-Green-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Add Green to Your Salt Lineup', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Sprinkle: Add Some Green To Your Salt Lineup', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-09T19:28:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46856830-3f8f-5b1b-8bb6-4a0a7723cbc2', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46856830-3f8f-5b1b-8bb6-4a0a7723cbc2'}, {'abstract': 'A new documentary on Champagne explores its roots in France, but also how sparkling wines have flourished across the English Channel thanks to global warming.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/09/dining/champagne-movie.html', 'snippet': 'A new documentary on Champagne explores its roots in France, but also how sparkling wines have flourished across the English Channel thanks to global warming.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new documentary, “Sparkling: The Story of Champagne,” is a lush valentine to the French bubbly, with history, profiles of many of the producers, pithy quotes about the wine, its celebrity connections (notably Winston Churchill) and tidbits about Champagne in the United States. But perhaps more compelling is its coverage of English sparkling wines, a less well-known topic. Climate change has turned the south of England into sparkling wine country, something the French acknowledge onscreen, with admiration; some French producers now own vineyards in England. But whether France is ready to credit England with having discovered champagne before Dom Pérignon’s 17th-century epiphany, a controversy the film examines, or whether James Bond will forgo his Bollinger for Hush Heath Estate, one of the English houses in the film, remains to be seen.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-Film/09Burner-Film-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 296, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-Film/09Burner-Film-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 296}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-Film/09Burner-Film-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 506, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-Film/09Burner-Film-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1011, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-Film/09Burner-Film-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/11/dining/09Burner-Film/09Burner-Film-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/1