'value': 'Shortages', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Workplace Hazards and Violations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-10T09:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Hoffman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoffman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4df3ecf8-90d2-5803-8f35-8c34f568d3b7', 'word_count': 1102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4df3ecf8-90d2-5803-8f35-8c34f568d3b7'}, {'abstract': 'First, I rinse my mouth, to loosen low-hanging fruit, and then spit out these easy spoils. Then the proper excavation begins.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/10/magazine/flossing.html', 'snippet': 'First, I rinse my mouth, to loosen low-hanging fruit, and then spit out these easy spoils. Then the proper excavation begins.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Flossing is a game I take seriously. I would rather floss than shop. I would rather floss than write. I would rather floss than fornicate. No post-orgasmic tristesse for the flosser, who seeks extraction, not ecstasy. Once, I bought a red Miu Miu peacoat that made me look like Ethel Merman, but flossing never makes me look like anyone but myself. It solidifies identity; it sews you into your predicament. I’m never Robert Redford or Ruth Buzzi when I floss. I’m always this particular mouth, mine, and this specific, tilted set of teeth, too small, too jagged, too staggered, like leaning housefronts in a German Expressionist stage set.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'ur