en the H.R. Department Approved', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-13T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nina Reyes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reyes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0949a68d-dcfe-567e-8a4a-fd697e83b02f', 'word_count': 597, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0949a68d-dcfe-567e-8a4a-fd697e83b02f'}, {'abstract': 'I like living in a place where not everyone thinks the same way I do. Or at least I used to.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/13/opinion/Alabama-Covid-vaccine-politics.html', 'snippet': 'I like living in a place where not everyone thinks the same way I do. Or at least I used to.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AUBURN, Ala. — When I had just moved here — six years ago and a lifetime ago — I was shopping at Publix, wheeling my cart out to the car. My baby sat in the buggy. I hit a bump, and the bottle of sparkling water I’d just bought skittered onto the ground, exploding. A young man in a Publix uniform ran up; I anticipated frustration (I’d made quite a mess), but instead he apologized for my mistake and ran inside to get another bottle to replace it.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/18/opinion/18disclafani-inyt/13disclafani-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 578, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/18/opinion/18disclafani-inyt/13disclafani-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 578}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/18/opinion/18disclafani-inyt/13disclafani-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 987, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/18/opinion/18disclafani-inyt/13disclafani-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1973, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/18/opinion/18disclafani-inyt/13disclafani-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/18/opinion/18disclafani-inyt/13disclafani-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/18/opinion/18disclafani-inyt/13disclafani-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Vaccine Refusers Are Testing My Love of the South', 'kicker': 'Guest Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vaccine Refusers Are Testing My Love of the South', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rumors and Misinformation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-13T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anton DiSclafani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anton', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DiSclafani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d06cebc9-9099-5457-9e6a-e57c13029e24', 'word_count': 1354, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d06cebc9-9099-5457-9e6a-e57c13029e24'}, {'abstract': 'Political turmoil and the pandemic pushed Eric Ostrow and Scott Linder to finally get married.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/13/fashion/weddings/eric-ostrow-scott-linder-wedding.html', 'snippet': 'Political turmoil and the pandemic pushed Eric Ostrow and Scott Linder to finally get married.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If not for the political turmoil of the last few years, Eric Ostrow and Scott Linder would probably not be planning to celebrate their 28th anniversary in December as a newly married couple. “I always used to tease Eric: We’ll just be like Kurt Russell and Goldie Hawn — no need to get married,” Mr. Linder said. But the threat they felt was strong enough that they decided to reconsider their position. “The past four years were terrifying for us,” said Mr. Ostrow, the director of client services at the law firm Harmon Linder & Rogowsky in New York. “I did not feel safe as a gay man.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/15/fashion/15OstrowLinder/00OstrowLinder-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/15/fashion/15OstrowLinder/00OstrowLinder-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 480}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/15/fashion/15OstrowLinder/00OstrowLinder-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption':