e/55844db8-447a-50fb-be9a-071ea73976fd', 'word_count': 1157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55844db8-447a-50fb-be9a-071ea73976fd'}, {'abstract': 'Afghanistan’s capital fell to the Taliban far faster than many had imagined it would, leaving most Afghans with no way out.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/15/world/asia/kabul-afghanistan.html', 'snippet': 'Afghanistan’s capital fell to the Taliban far faster than many had imagined it would, leaving most Afghans with no way out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The citizens of Kabul were given reassurances that they would be safe, that a deal had been struck to avoid a full-fledged attack by the Taliban on their city. But for many Afghans, the scenes playing out around them on Sunday in their capital told another story.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In an Overrun Kabul, Many Have No Place to Run', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Single Day, Taliban Push Transforms  Life in Kabul', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kabul (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-15T22:33:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carlotta Gall, Christina Goldbaum, Thomas Gibbons-Neff and Ruhullah Khapalwak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carlotta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Christina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gibbons-Neff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}, {'firstname': 'Ruhullah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Khapalwak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 4}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77f89ad9-248e-5631-894b-8d875a5be8cb', 'word_count': 566, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77f89ad9-248e-5631-894b-8d875a5be8cb'}, {'abstract': 'After 20 years of diplomacy and support, American officials are now contemplating the harrowing question of whether — and how — they might engage with a Taliban government in Afghanistan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/15/us/politics/biden-taliban-afghanistan.html', 'snippet': 'After 20 years of diplomacy and support, American officials are now contemplating the harrowing question of whether — and how — they might engage with a Taliban government in Afghanistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The fall of Kabul on Sunday left the Biden administration facing the once-unthinkable prospect of whether, and how, to engage with a Taliban-led government in Afghanistan’s capital — or cede all influence in the country to an extremist group that brutalized Afghans and harbored Osama bin Laden as he planned attacks on America.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/16/us/politics/16dc-diplo-print1/merlin_193297425_2a34394d-3b13-45f6-bd7a-94fc9736c9ce-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/16/us/politics/16dc-diplo-print1/merlin_193297425_2a34394d-3b13-45f6-bd7a-94fc9736c9ce-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/16/us/politics/16dc-diplo-print1/merlin_1932