e': '2021-08-17T11:17:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eshe Nelson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eshe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nelson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c17873f-a7ae-5723-b249-56055072b67a', 'word_count': 508, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c17873f-a7ae-5723-b249-56055072b67a'}, {'abstract': 'The wildfire is one of at least six large ones burning in Northern California. High winds, low humidity and searing temperatures exacerbate the risk that fire will spread.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/17/us/dixie-fire-updates-janesville-susanville.html', 'snippet': 'The wildfire is one of at least six large ones burning in Northern California. High winds, low humidity and searing temperatures exacerbate the risk that fire will spread.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Dixie fire, the second-largest in California’s recorded history, was wheeling on Tuesday toward one of the biggest urban targets in its path: the city of Susanville, population 15,000.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dixie fire nears communities in the Sierra Nevada foothills.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Small City Is in Big Trouble as Dixie Fire Bears Down', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wildfires', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fires and Firefighters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cal Fire', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pacific Gas and Electric Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lassen County (Calif)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sierra Nevada Region (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Shasta County (Calif)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-17T11:55:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Express', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Ives and Eduardo Medina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ives', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Eduardo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Medina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/55a70ee1-267a-5f80-bfd9-69fc1e7527c1', 'word_count': 645, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55a70ee1-267a-5f80-bfd9-69fc1e7527c1'}, {'abstract': 'Over centuries, the legendary creature has become woven into the cultural imagination — and still fascinates as a symbol of the untamed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/17/t-magazine/unicorns-culture-myth.html', 'snippet': 'Over centuries, the legendary creature has become woven into the cultural imagination — and still fascinates as a symbol of the untamed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the fourth century B.C., a Greek physician named Ctesias described an animal that would become known as the unicorn: a large, pale blue-eyed ass with a crimson head and a horn of white, red and black, found only in India. Likely created from stories he heard while stationed in Persia (modern-day Iran), Ctesias’ “Indica” — a written catalog of the unknown world — shimmers with the sublime and the absurd: notes on a race of people with a single leg; descriptions of chimeric beasts. But it was the unicorn, possessor of what Ctesias described as a cinnabar-red anklebone, that remains his most enduring contribution. (The scholar Chris Lavers, author of 2009’s “The Natural History of Unicorns,” has written that Ctesias assembled the unicorn out of three animals indigenous to the region: the Indian rhinoceros, whose horn is associated with medicinal properties; the goatlike, horned chiru; and the reddish-and-white-colored kiang, a large wild ass.)', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '76', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-unicorns-slide-CUKW/18tmag-unicorns-slide-CUKW-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 781, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-unicorns-slide-CUKW/18tmag-unicorns-slide-CUKW-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 781}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-unicorns-slide-CUKW/18tmag-unicorns-slide-CUKW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 787, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-unicorns-slide-CUKW/18tmag-unicorns-slide-CUKW-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1725, 'width': 1325, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-unicorns-slide-CUKW/18tmag-unicorns-slide-CUKW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-unicorns-slide-CUKW/18tmag-unicorns-slide-CUKW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-unicorns-slide-CUKW/18tmag-unicorns-slide-CUKW-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name'