al': 'By Joe Drape', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drape', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c8e4e58-5fdb-57c2-a887-c6b579f002cd', 'word_count': 845, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c8e4e58-5fdb-57c2-a887-c6b579f002cd'}, {'abstract': 'Scientists are developing devices and clothing that make running and walking easier and more enjoyable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/18/well/move/exoskeleton-suit-walking-running.html', 'snippet': 'Scientists are developing devices and clothing that make running and walking easier and more enjoyable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Those of us who are slow or reluctant runners or walkers might soon be able to slip on a lightweight, lower-body exoskeleton and up the speed and ease of our exercise, according to several new studies examining the effects of these high-tech robotic devices Personal, or wearable, exoskeletons, usually amalgamated from motors, cables, straps, springs and ingenuity, can shoulder a substantial portion of the work when we walk or run, the new studies show, potentially allowing us to move much faster or farther. They can even harvest energy from the movement — almost enough to power a cellphone.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/24/well/18sci-physed-exoskeleton1/18sci-physed-exoskeleton1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/24/well/18sci-physed-exoskeleton1/18sci-physed-exoskeleton1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 403}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/24/well/18sci-physed-exoskeleton1/18sci-physed-exoskelet