e': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Seibert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seibert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c68a1ba-91ae-5964-b9b4-3ce1c1327399', 'word_count': 580, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c68a1ba-91ae-5964-b9b4-3ce1c1327399'}, {'abstract': 'He juxtaposed the mundane and the exotic, transforming ordinary objects into the desirable — an approach he took in his still-life images as well as in fashion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/18/style/hiro-dead.html', 'snippet': 'He juxtaposed the mundane and the exotic, transforming ordinary objects into the desirable — an approach he took in his still-life images as well as in fashion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yasuhiro Wakabayashi, the Japanese American photographer known as Hiro, whose fashion and still-life images captured a relentlessly inventive vision of American life that critics likened to those of his idol and mentor, Richard Avedon, died on Sunday at his country home in Erwinna, Pa. He was 90. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/20/obituaries/18Hiro/merlin_193400172_40533442-3de8-4042-9d14-3895838b5e07-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 794, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/20/obituaries/18Hiro/merlin_193400172_40533442-3de8-4042-9d14-3895838b5e07-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 794}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/20/obituaries/18Hiro/merlin_193400172_40533442-3de8-4042-9d14-3895838b5e07-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 775, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': '