rson': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b795f977-ae8b-53ed-a2df-c715adb89b64', 'word_count': 967, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b795f977-ae8b-53ed-a2df-c715adb89b64'}, {'abstract': 'Inspired by ancient animal figurines, contemporary potters are making creatures that reflect the irregular beauty and magic of the natural world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/19/t-magazine/ceramic-animal-figurines.html', 'snippet': 'Inspired by ancient animal figurines, contemporary potters are making creatures that reflect the irregular beauty and magic of the natural world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ARTISTS HAVE MADE animals from clay for nearly as long as history has been recorded. Among the artifacts unearthed at the legendary 1983 archaeological dig in Jordan’s Ain Ghazal were a number of small creatures from the Neolithic period, some of the earliest objects of their kind ever found. At first, archaeologists wondered if the reddish, palm-size bulls they discovered had been children’s toys, but they soon realized that their function was largely spiritual — magical, even: They were totems at the site’s temple altar.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '132', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-figurines-slide-6CBR/20tmag-figurines-slide-6CBR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-figurines-slide-6CBR/20tmag-figurines-slide-6CBR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 363}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/20/t-magazine/20tmag-figurines-slide-6CBR/20tmag-figurines-slide-6CBR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 620, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'su