, 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Americans Abroad', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-20T11:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Lordi and Manuel Obadia-Wills', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lordi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Manuel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Obadia-Wills', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51bf5ad9-aa49-5a79-a78b-1a2bd698f37f', 'word_count': 3707, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51bf5ad9-aa49-5a79-a78b-1a2bd698f37f'}, {'abstract': 'The Afghanistan National Institute of Music became a symbol of the country’s changing identity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/20/arts/music/taliban-music-afghanistan-school.html', 'snippet': 'The Afghanistan National Institute of Music became a symbol of the country’s changing identity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For more than a decade, the Afghanistan National Institute of Music has stood as a symbol of the country’s changing identity. The school trained hundreds of young artists, many of them orphans and street hawkers, in artistic traditions that were once forbidden by the Taliban. It formed an all-female orchestra that performed widely in Afghanistan and abroad.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/20/arts/20afghanistan-culture-1/merlin_155906088_a0540855-d74c-479d-a67f-e5f72bcd6729-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/20/arts/20afghanistan-culture-1/merlin_155906088_a0540855-d74c-479d-a67f-e5f72bcd6729-article