Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-22T21:09:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'NYTNow', 'section_name': 'Briefing', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amelia Nierenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amelia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nierenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/17c55cde-a82a-5a62-b7d3-3e9b5bda5570', 'word_count': 1275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17c55cde-a82a-5a62-b7d3-3e9b5bda5570'}, {'abstract': 'This summer, the journalist Kiana Hayeri set out to photograph Afghans who grew up after the U.S. invasion in 2001. Instead, she wound up documenting the end of life as they knew it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/22/magazine/afghanistan-photos.html', 'snippet': 'This summer, the journalist Kiana Hayeri set out to photograph Afghans who grew up after the U.S. invasion in 2001. Instead, she wound up documenting the end of life as they knew it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gul Ahmad went to school only until fifth grade. After his father died, his mother remarried. When Gul Ahmad was 12, his stepfather forced him to go to Iran to work and bring money back to the family. At the time of the photo above, the boys had traveled to Iran, hoping to find work, but were deported back to Afghanistan and waiting at a center for migrants to be reunited with their families. When Hayeri met them, they were constantly on their phones. Gul Ahmad played games, and Karim watched videos of young Iranian women smoking hookah and playing guitar.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/29/magazine/29mag-afghanistan-33-02-copy/29mag-afghanistan-33-02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08