t': 1048, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e98417a-fb3a-5454-a66d-64c2500c4e1c'}, {'abstract': 'They employed social media to cultivate an image of strength within Afghanistan while projecting an air of legitimacy to the outside world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/23/opinion/taliban-afghanistan-social-media.html', 'snippet': 'They employed social media to cultivate an image of strength within Afghanistan while projecting an air of legitimacy to the outside world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Aug. 14, a day before armed fighters swarmed into Kabul, a Twitter account for one of the Taliban’s magazines posted a video of six nervous Afghan government soldiers sitting in a truck surrounded by Taliban warriors. The post included a snippet of text, in Pashto, one of the two main languages of Afghanistan: “While the mujahedeen behave generously to soldiers, the children of the village threw stones at them and called them dogs. That’s what happens in response to their atrocities.” The same day, a spokesman for the Taliban posted another Twitter message, this time in English, promising that the group would create “a secure environment” for all diplomats, embassies and nonprofits, both domestic and international. It ended with the Arabic benediction “Inshallah” (God willing).', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/25/opinion/sunday/21Stengel/21Stengel-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/25/opinion/sunday/21Stengel/21Stengel-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/25/opinion/sunday/21Stengel/21Stengel-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}},