/24SKIBBA-COMBO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/24/books/24SKIBBA-COMBO/24SKIBBA-COMBO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/24/books/24SKIBBA-COMBO/24SKIBBA-COMBO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/24/books/24SKIBBA-COMBO/24SKIBBA-COMBO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When the Big Bang Was Just a Theory', 'kicker': 'Nonfiction', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With a Bang', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Space and Astronomy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Physics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stars and Galaxies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Halpern, Paul (1961- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Flashes of Creation: George Gamow, Fred Hoyle, and the Great Big Bang Debate (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hoyle, Fred', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gamow, George', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-24T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ramin Skibba', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ramin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Skibba', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6f4eb65-849f-5da1-b877-1a91eaaf396f', 'word_count': 857, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6f4eb65-849f-5da1-b877-1a91eaaf396f'}, {'abstract': 'There’s no greater pleasure than biting into a slice of a raw red onion and tasting its stinging sharpness.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/24/magazine/raw-onions-are-the-best-food-let-me-explain.html', 'snippet': 'There’s no greater pleasure than biting into a slice of a raw red onion and tasting its stinging sharpness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eight years ago, I spent my first 48 hours in the United States being driven around the New Jersey suburbs by an acquaintance of my father’s. In his eagerness to familiarize me with the superiority of his (and now my) surrogate nation, he took particular care to show me the fundamental institutions that, according to him, made America great: the colossal gates granting entry to New Jersey’s very own Mycenae, the Woodbridge outlet mall; a Dunkin’ doughnut shop; the tube outside a drive-in TD Bank that sucks in checks. Pride shimmered in his eyes, but he grew increasingly irritated when I could not muster much enthusiasm for iced coffee and doughnuts. (I did not have it in me to tell him that Dunkin’ also has outposts in New Delhi, the city I left behind.) It was not until I came face to face with a tower of grenade-size red onions while browsing the organic-produce section at a Wegmans later that day that I felt my detachment toward this new country begin to ease a little.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/29/magazine/29mag-LOR/29mag-LOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 437, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/29/magazine/29mag-LOR/29mag-LOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 437}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/29/magazine/29mag-LOR/29mag-LOR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 745, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/29/magazine/29mag-LOR/29mag-LOR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1491, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/29/magazine/29mag-LOR/29mag-LOR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/29/magazine/29mag-LOR/29mag-LOR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/29/magazine/29mag-LOR/29mag-LOR-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Raw Onions Are the Best Food. Let Me Explain.', 'kicker': 'Letter of Recommendation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Raw Onions ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Onions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indian Food (Cuisine)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-24T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Iva Dixit', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Iva', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dixit', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/179c56ca-25c6-5430-bca2-a79cb3de56e4', 'word_count': 933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/179c56ca-25c6-5430-bca2-a79cb3de56e4'}, {'abstract': 'I’ve spent my life preparing for the biggest games a person could play, but nothing prepares you to be a caregiver who has to watch a loved one slip away.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/24/opinion/tom-coughlin-wife-psp.html', 'snippet': 'I’ve spent my life preparing for the biggest games a person could play, but nothing prepares you to be a caregiver who has to watch a loved one slip away.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Someone recently asked me why my wife, Judy, isn’t in any of the photos from our Jay Fund Foundation events, and it was with a heavy heart that I had to explain. For the past year, I’ve been torn between protecting my wife’s dignity and privacy and sharing some deeply personal and sad news. It’s only after some reflection that I’ve come to the conclusion that what my family and I are experiencing may be helpful for others to read.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/26/opinion/24coughlin/24coughlin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 914, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/26/opinion/24coughlin/24coughlin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 914}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/26/opinion/24coughlin/24coughlin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 673, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/26/opinion/24coughlin/24coughlin-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1851, 'width': 1215, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/26/opinion/24coughlin/24coughlin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/26/opinion/24coughlin/24coughlin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/26/opinion/24coughlin/24coughlin-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nothing Could Prepare Me for Watching My Wife Slip Away', 'kicker': 'Guest Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Watching My Wife Slip Away', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-24T09:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Coughlin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Coughlin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b3f4f54-8234-5fcb-af75-e51f934e2dfb', 'word_count': 835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b3f4f54-8234-5fcb-af75-e51f934e2dfb'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on an invasion of privacy and reporting misconduct at work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/24/magazine/therapy-roommates-ethics.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on an invasion of privacy and reporting misconduct at work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My therapist is no longer seeing patients in person, so I’ve been attending therapy sessions at home in my room. Recently, one of my roommates told me that she had been listening to my sessions for weeks and heard me mention her name and the name of our other roommate. Now both roommates have told me I will not be allowed to have my sessions in the apartment anymore. They asked me to do them in a coffee shop or the public library (which is still closed in my city because of Covid). I could get a white-noise machine, but at this point I’m scared that they may hear a part of my session and gang up on me again.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/29/magazine/29mag-ethicist/29mag-ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/29/magazine/29mag-ethicist/29mag-ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/29/magazine/29mag-ethicist/29mag-ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/29/magazine/29mag-ethicist/29mag-ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1148, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/29/magazine/29mag-ethicist/29mag-ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/29/magazine/29mag-ethicist/29mag-ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/29/magazine/29mag-ethicist/29mag-ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'My Roommates Have Been Listening to My Therapy Sessions. Is That OK?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'My Roommates Have Been Listening to My Therapy Sessions. Is That OK?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Content Type: Service', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-24T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kwame Anthony Appiah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kwame', 'middlename': 'Anthony', 'lastname': 'Appiah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/335b00f2-4aff-53ff-8624-4a6ab6f927e8', 'word_count': 1406, 'uri': 'nyt://article/335b00f2-4aff-53ff-8624-4a6ab6f927e8'}, {'abstract': 'Shootings are still well above prepandemic levels. But this summer has been considerably less violent than last.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/24/nyregion/nyc-shootings-guns.html', 'snippet': 'Shootings are still well above prepandemic levels. But this summer has been considerably less violent than last.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dozens of people were gathered outside on a recent Sunday night, listening to music and partying in the Bedford-Stuyvesant neighborhood of Brooklyn, when two men walked up and opened fire, wounding eight. Ten minutes later, three teenagers were shot nearby in Cypress Hills. And the same night, three men in their 20s were wounded by gunfire in Springfield Gardens, Queens.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/23/nyregion/00crimestats2/merlin_174524811_a38e6d90-d1b4-42c0-a9e7-b02f086bade4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/23/nyregion/00crimestats2/merlin_174524811_a38e6d90-d1b4-42c0-a9e7-b02f086bade4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/23/nyregion/00crimestats2/merlin_174524811_a38e6d90-d1b4-42c0-a9e7-b02f086bade4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/23/nyregion/00crimestats2/merlin_174524811_a38e6d90-d1b4-42c0-a9e7-b02f086bade4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/23/nyregion/00crimestats2/00crimestats2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/23/nyregion/00crimestats2/00crimestats2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/23/nyregion/00crimestats2/00crimestats2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is New York’s Wave of Gun Violence Receding? Experts See Reason for Hope.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Shootings Appear to Ebb, Experts See Reason to Hope', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Firearms', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders, Attempted Murders and Homicides', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-08-24T09:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Troy Closson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Troy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Closson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d54c16d-44b3-5cdb-b240-9a0e5b2e5acd', 'word_count': 1353, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d54c16d-44b3-5cdb-b240-9a0e5b2e5acd'}, {'abstract': 'After much holdover, nuptials are back on for 2022 and invites are piling up. But just because you’re ready to get out and celebrate doesn’t mean your wallet is.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/24/style/prepping-for-the-year-of-many-many-weddings.html', 'snippet': 'After much holdover, nuptials are back on for 2022 and invites are piling up. But just because you’re ready to get out and celebrate doesn’t mean your wallet is.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the third wedding was added to our 2022 calendar, I was delighted to have another joyful event after a year and half of small, limited celebrations. When the sixth friend’s “We’re engaged!” announcement appeared on my Instagram feed, I worried but shrugged it off. After all, why spend energy thinking about all those logistics when my fiancé and I were busy planning our own 2022 wedding, anyway?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/24/fashion/24WeddingTravelOverload/24WeddingTravelOverload-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge