itics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Fandos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fandos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69e5a316-43c6-5c12-9e2e-94d7b107c07f', 'word_count': 1307, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69e5a316-43c6-5c12-9e2e-94d7b107c07f'}, {'abstract': 'The Pentagon has increased the number of flights, but questions remain about whether the military can sustain the pace as the deadline to end the operation draws near.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/24/us/politics/us-military-evacuations-kabul.html', 'snippet': 'The Pentagon has increased the number of flights, but questions remain about whether the military can sustain the pace as the deadline to end the operation draws near.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — With the Aug. 31 deadline for a U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan rapidly approaching, the Pentagon has sharply ramped up the speed of evacuations from the Kabul airport, flying out 21,600 people in 24 hours, Defense Department officials said Tuesday. But bottlenecks in the system, and President Biden’s insistence that all troops leave the country by the end of the month, may prevent the military from keeping that pace.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/24/us/24dc-military1/merlin_193638312_cd16bb29-9ad4-4520-ab36-5f5dcc8398dc-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/24/us/24dc-military1/merlin_193638312_cd16bb29-9ad4-4520-ab36-5f5dcc8398dc-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/24/us/24dc-military1/m