ddlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c897938-6d44-55ea-ad8d-64eb53ce9c91', 'word_count': 271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c897938-6d44-55ea-ad8d-64eb53ce9c91'}, {'abstract': 'We need to learn to talk about climate change in a way that isn’t so politically charged.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/30/opinion/tennessee-natural-disasters-climate-change.html', 'snippet': 'We need to learn to talk about climate change in a way that isn’t so politically charged.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NASHVILLE — By now you’ve probably seen photos of the apocalyptic flooding in Middle Tennessee’s Dickson, Hickman, Houston and Humphreys Counties on Aug. 21, though it’s understandable if you scrolled right on past without really looking. There’s a déjà vu quality to such images. Cars lifted by some unstoppable force and returned to earth cattywampus. Houses at odds with their foundations, or just gone. Holes where roads used to be. The debris field of a bombing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/01/opinion/30renkl-second/30renkl-second-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/01/opinion/30renkl-second/30renkl-second-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/01/opinion/30renkl-second/30renkl-second-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/01/opinion/30renkl-second/30renkl-second-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1360, 'width': 2048, 'subTy